# printer-interrupt.ti59
#
# The TI-59 "printer interrupt" in its smallest form. The program prints
# "A0" (at the right end of the line) twice: once normally, and once cut off
# after the first 2 of its 7 dot rows. rom/printerinterrupt.md explains the hack;
# examples/historic/texas-print.ti59 and examples/historic/printer-quirks.ti59 are the 1982
# PPX Exchange programs that use it.
#
# Loading the file runs everything automatically (KEYSTROKES below). By hand:
#
#   1 + 48 EE 12 +/- =     X := 1.000000000048 exactly
#   GTO 012  SST  0  STO   fast-mode hack: Prg Src := 8, stop at step 007
#   LRN  2nd Ins  LRN      step 008 becomes the pseudo-code 1F, 009 RTN
#   CLR  RST               back to normal (Prg Src 0)
#   GTO 017  R/S           run the program
#
# No Master Library module and no special partition are needed. The method
# replaces the article's [9] [Op] [17] [Pgm] [12] [SBR] [444] [R/S] [P/R]
# sequence and gives the same 1F and RTN (headless check, also on
# texas-print.ti59, whose printout stays the same).
#
# HOW THE CODE IS MADE
#
# 1. St flg at 012 is the fast-mode hack (rom/fastmode.md): SST runs it, the
#    digit 0 and STO end its operand early, and X is copied into the status
#    register. X = 1.000000000048 sets Prg Src 8 and step 004. Its leading 1
#    sets flag 4; a leading 4, 5, 8 or 9 would make LRN and Ins print junk
#    lines.
# 2. A non-zero Prg Src starts a run. The step buffer still holds 008-015,
#    positioned at 012, so steps 005-007 run 013-015: 013 is swallowed, 014
#    types 0, 015 is R/S. The run stops at step 007 with Prg Src still 8.
# 3. LRN advances to 008. That starts a new block, so the buffer is reloaded,
#    and with Prg Src 8 it comes from ROM constant row 17 (04 65 43 01 ...)
#    instead of RAM.
# 4. Ins computes (RAM - buffer) + (buffer shifted up one step). Digit 0 of
#    the register is hexadecimal in the CPU, so step 008 = 23 (lnx) - 04 =
#    1F, and step 009 = 53 (() - 65 - borrow + 04 = 92 (RTN). Steps 010-015
#    get garbage, step 016 receives the R/S, and everything from 016 on
#    moves up one step (the listing below shows memory BEFORE the Ins).
#
# HOW THE INTERRUPT WORKS
#
# A = 0 Op 05 1F: Op 05 starts printing the line, and the printer prints
# its 7 dot rows on its own (one stroke per 508 instruction cycles). Meanwhile step 008 executes.
# The keycode dispatcher (ROM 0F53) turns the two digits of 1F into a jump
# to 0EF1 without checking them. 0EF1 is the tail of the number-print
# routine, three instructions before PRT.GO (0EFC), the "line finished,
# advance paper" signal. PRT.GO arriving while the printer is busy stops it:
# 1020 instruction cycles have passed, two 508-cycle strokes: 2 rows are on paper. The
# code then continues at step 000 (INV SBR), which returns to 009 (RTN),
# which returns from A. In the app the second line shows only 2 of its 7
# dot rows; the text in a headless printout is always complete.
#
# The article's rules follow from this: the code only goes at steps 8k
# (only digit 0 of a register is hexadecimal), 000 must hold INV SBR, X
# must be 0 without EE when it runs (here: the 0 at 005; CLR ends the EE
# mode of the hack constant), and all labels must come before it.

MODEL: TI-59
PRINTER: on

PROGRAM:
000  92  INV SBR   # the interrupt continues here: return to 009
001  00  0
002  00  0
003  76  Lbl
004  11  A
005  00  0         # display must be 0
006  69  Op
007  05  05        # start printing the line …
008  23  lnx       # … becomes 1F: the interrupt
009  53  (         # becomes 92 RTN
010  00  0
011  00  0
012  86  St flg    # hack trigger (overwritten by the Ins)
013  00  0         # swallowed by the hack
014  00  0         # makes X displayable before the R/S
015  91  R/S       # stops the hack's run (moves to 016)
# From here on, each step moves up one during the Ins: 016 runs as 017.
016  01  1         # 1301 = codes 13 01 = "A0"
017  03  3
018  00  0
019  01  1
020  69  Op
021  04  04        # into the last 5 columns of the line
022  69  Op
023  05  05        # print the line in full
024  11  A         # print it again, interrupted
025  98  Adv
026  91  R/S

KEYSTROKES:
82          # 1
85          # +
72          # 4
63          # 8
52          # EE
82          # 1
83          # 2
94          # +/-
95          # =      X = 1.000000000048
61          # GTO
92          # 0
82          # 1
83          # 2      → step 012
41          # SST    St flg executes, operand pending
92          # 0      first operand digit
42          # STO    ends it: Prg Src 8, run stops at 007
31          # LRN    008, buffer loaded from ROM row 17
21 41       # Ins    008 := 1F, 009 := RTN
31          # LRN
25          # CLR
81          # RST    Prg Src 0
61          # GTO
92          # 0
82          # 1
62          # 7      → step 017
91          # R/S    print both lines
